# Jira ticketing rules (Project AA)

When the user asks for a Jira ticket, produce a **ready-to-create** ticket with consistent structure and minimal back-and-forth.

## Defaults
- **Project key**: `AA` (override if user specifies a different project/key)
- **Issue type**: `Task` (unless user says Bug/Story/Epic)
- **Description format**: plain text (not ADF JSON)
- **Acceptance Criteria**: bullet checklist
- **Labels**: default include `AdvancedAnalytics` (override if user specifies different labels)
- **Assignee**: default assign to `Sophie Wilson` (override if user requests different)
- **Story points**: **always** set when drafting a ticket. Use an explicit number in the **Story points** section. If the user only gives a **size label**, map with **Assumption** using this default scale (override if the user names different team values): trivial `1`, small `2`, medium `3`, medium/large `5`, large `8`, extra-large `13`. If estimation is impossible, write **Story points: TBD** and say why.

## Verbatim data request (description line 1)
When the user provides a **verbatim data / analytics request** (spreadsheet row, intake field, Slack/email paste, etc.), the **plain-text description body** (what becomes Jira `description` / `-Description`) **must** start with **exactly** that text as the **first line**, wrapped in **double quotes** (`"..."`). **Do not paraphrase, correct spelling, or trim meaning** inside the quotes—only remove accidental leading/trailing outer whitespace on the pasted cell if clearly spurious. After the closing quote, use **one blank line**, then continue with **Background / context** and the rest of the template. If **no** source request string was given, **omit** the quoted line (do not invent a quote).

## Output format (copy/paste friendly)
Always output these sections (omit only if not applicable):
- **Summary**: short, action-oriented
- **Quoted data request** (first line of description only, when user supplied verbatim text): `"exact pasted request"` then blank line—see **Verbatim data request** above
- **Background / context**: why this matters + any links provided
- **Problem / goal**: what outcome is needed
- **Scope**: what’s included
- **Out of scope**: what is explicitly excluded
- **Acceptance Criteria**: bullet checklist, testable
- **Dependencies / blockers**
- **Risks / notes**
- **Test notes**: how to verify + any data/analytics expectations
- **Story points**: integer (or `TBD` with reason); must align with auto-create `-StoryPoints` when using the script
- **AI disclaimer**: add at the very bottom: `Disclaimer: This ticket was created with AI assistance.`

## Bug-specific additions
If the user indicates it’s a bug, also include:
- **Steps to reproduce**
- **Expected vs actual**
- **Environment**: app/version/browser/permissions (as applicable)
- **Impact / severity**

## Auto-create guidance (repo script)
If the user wants the ticket created automatically, provide the exact command using this repo’s script:

`powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\jira-create.ps1 -ProjectKey AA -IssueType <Task|Bug|Story> -Summary "<...>" -Description "<...>" -Labels AdvancedAnalytics -Assignee "sophie.wilson" -StoryPoints <n>`

Notes:
- **Always** pass `-StoryPoints <n>` when the draft has a numeric estimate (`-1` omits; only use omit when story points are genuinely **TBD**). If the API cannot set the field, the script warns and you set points in the Jira UI.
- When using **auto-create** with a **multiline** `-Description`, run from the same PowerShell session: `$d = @'...'@; & .\scripts\jira-create.ps1 ... -Description $d` (avoid `powershell -File ... -Description $d`, which can split arguments). Put the **quoted verbatim data request** as the **first line** of `$d` when applicable.
- Keep `-Summary` under ~120 chars if possible.
- If the description is long, keep it plain text with clear headings.
- If the user specifies labels/assignee/project key, reflect those overrides in both the draft and the auto-create command.
- If an Epic is provided (or defaulted for the project), always set the **Epic Link** so the ticket appears under the Epic tab.
- If both Epic and Initiative are provided, set both links explicitly.

## Quality bar
- Don’t invent requirements. If a key detail is missing, make a reasonable assumption and mark it clearly as **Assumption**.
- Acceptance criteria must be verifiable (avoid vague “works” / “looks good”).
